=== Allergen Discipline at Catered Occasions

Caterers on the Approved Catering Directory must supply an allergen matrix with every delivery, and the host keeps it with the delivery note until the occasion has ended. Platters that arrive without labelling are not to be opened; the host contacts the Travel Desk, which arranges a replacement from a second directory vendor. Where a guest has declared a severe allergy, the host confirms in the event note that separate utensils and a dedicated serving point were arranged. A host guessing at an ingredient list has, on two documented occasions, produced an afternoon in a hospital waiting room and a very long incident review.

#card-dont("Order unlabelled platters for mixed groups")[
  Never accept a delivery whose contents cannot be matched to an allergen matrix. An unlabelled buffet is not a convenience; it is an unmanaged risk. If the caterer cannot label, the Travel Desk selects another vendor and records the failure in the vendor's quality file.
]

=== Cultural and Religious Catering

The Travel Desk maintains a shared festival and fasting calendar covering all offices, and hosts check it before fixing the date of any catered occasion. Scheduling a mandatory workshop over a major fasting period is discouraged; where the business need is genuine, the occasion is moved or the format adjusted so that no attendee must choose between participation and observance. Shared kitchen areas carry signage on segregated storage and utensils where the headcount includes employees with religious dietary requirements. Questions about specific observances go to People Operations, which answers them directly.

== The Approved Catering Directory

The Travel Desk maintains the Approved Catering Directory, segmented by cuisine, city, delivery capability, and allergen handling. Ordering from a directory vendor is strongly preferred for internal events: directory vendors carry negotiated service terms, verified food-safety certification, and a quality history the Travel Desk can consult when something goes wrong. Ad-hoc ordering from an unknown vendor is permitted for solo and small meals but not for events with more than eight attendees.

